Debian package maintainers should create the sources with the
-i -I and -Idoc_tmp.* parameters to exclude unneeded svn-files
and temporary doc-files, otherwise the sources might become
really large.
If you want to build without docs (or have no internet-access
during the build), you can comment out the call
to "./updateDocs.sh" in the rules-script, and remove the entries
for the doc-packages and the build-dependency for "pngnq" and "wget" in
"debian/control".


Building the Debian packages on Debian or Ubuntu
-------------------------------------------------

The installable distribution is a set of .deb packages. They are built
from this tree with dpkg-buildpackage. debian/rules configures the
autotools build with --prefix=/usr and --with-contrib-plugins=all.
Run the commands below from the root of the source tree.

1. Install the packaging tools and the wxWidgets development package.
   ./bootstrap needs wxwin.m4 from that package before the later
   "apt build-dep" step installs the rest of the dependencies.

    sudo apt update
    sudo apt install build-essential fakeroot devscripts debhelper cdbs \
        automake autoconf libtool pkg-config

   Also install the wxWidgets package for this release. GTK is pulled in
   with it:

    Debian stretch, buster (9, 10)      sudo apt install libwxgtk3.0-dev
    Debian bullseye (11),
    Ubuntu focal (20.04), jammy (22.04) sudo apt install libwxgtk3.0-gtk3-dev
    Newer Debian and Ubuntu releases    sudo apt install libwxgtk3.2-dev

2. Generate the autotools files, then debian/control. ./bootstrap deletes
   debian/control, so the setup script must run after it:

    ./bootstrap
    ./debian/setup_control.sh detect

   The setup script reads /etc/os-release and fills debian/control from
   debian/control.in. It selects the wxWidgets and GTK build dependencies
   for the running release:

    Debian stretch, buster (9, 10)     libwxgtk3.0-dev, libgtk2.0-dev
    Debian bullseye (11),
    Ubuntu focal (20.04), jammy (22.04) libwxgtk3.0-gtk3-dev, libgtk-3-dev
    Newer Debian and Ubuntu releases   libwxgtk3.2-dev, libgtk-3-dev

   To force a codename instead of detecting one, pass it as the argument,
   for example: ./debian/setup_control.sh jammy

3. Install the build dependencies listed in the generated debian/control
   (wxGTK, GTK, Boost, hunspell, bzip2, zlib, fontconfig, glib, and the
   rest of Build-Depends):

    sudo apt build-dep .

4. Build the binary packages only. -b skips the source tarball. -us -uc
   skips signing. The compile includes every contrib plugin and takes a
   while:

    DEB_BUILD_OPTIONS=parallel=$(nproc) dpkg-buildpackage -us -uc -b

   The .deb files are written in the parent directory. Their version comes
   from the first entry in debian/changelog. With the changelog entry
   "20.03svn12027" the files are named like:

    ../codeblocks_20.03svn12027_amd64.deb
    ../codeblocks-common_20.03svn12027_all.deb
    ../libcodeblocks0_20.03svn12027_amd64.deb
    ../codeblocks-contrib_20.03svn12027_amd64.deb
    ../codeblocks-contrib-common_20.03svn12027_all.deb
    ../codeblocks-libwxcontrib0_20.03svn12027_amd64.deb
    ../libwxsmithlib0_20.03svn12027_amd64.deb

   Architecture-independent packages use _all. The architecture suffix
   follows the machine (amd64, arm64, and so on). Debug-symbol, -dev, and
   -headers packages are produced as well.

5. Install the IDE and the contrib plugins with apt, so dependencies
   between the packages are resolved. From the parent directory, and
   substituting the version and architecture from the files just built:

    sudo apt install \
        ./codeblocks_20.03svn12027_amd64.deb \
        ./codeblocks-common_20.03svn12027_all.deb \
        ./libcodeblocks0_20.03svn12027_amd64.deb \
        ./codeblocks-contrib_20.03svn12027_amd64.deb \
        ./codeblocks-contrib-common_20.03svn12027_all.deb \
        ./codeblocks-libwxcontrib0_20.03svn12027_amd64.deb \
        ./libwxsmithlib0_20.03svn12027_amd64.deb

   Omit the codeblocks-contrib, codeblocks-contrib-common,
   codeblocks-libwxcontrib0, and libwxsmithlib0 packages to install the
   core IDE only. The *-dev, *-headers, and *-dbg packages are for
   writing plugins and for debugging.

If the build stops because debian/compat is missing, create that file
with a single line containing 10 and run dpkg-buildpackage again. Compat
level 13 rejects installed files that no debian/*.install list claims.
The codeblocks-headers binary package has no .install file, so that one
package can be empty. The IDE packages do not need it.